    "authorAssociation": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\r\n\r\n- [X] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\r\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\r\n\r\n### Module(s)\r\n\r\nmini.ai\r\n\r\n### Description\r\n\r\nHi, following on from https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/issues/261 I have been attempting to create text objects for `w`, `W` and `p`. I have made some headway with `w`, it currently looks like this (updated: 20230316):\r\n\r\n```lua\r\n          w = { '[^%w@_]*%f[%w@_]()()[%w@_]+()%s*()[^%w@_]*' },\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe problem is when I input `2yiw` for example, it seems to want to yank only the second \"word\". Default vim behaviour would be to select both the current \"word\" and the next one. I'm guessing this is to do with the way `v:count` is implemented for text objects?\r\n\r\nI don't know whether this is a bug or a feature, for example I know with bracket-type text objects, the behaviour of v:count with a count of 2 would be to select the brackets surrounding the ones you're in. (i.e. to expand outwards). But I think for things like `word`s and `WORD`s it makes sense to expand forwards.\r\n\r\nI'm wondering if it makes sense for text objects to be able to specify how they interact with `v:count`?",
    "bodyHTML": "<h3 dir=\"auto\">Contributing guidelines</h3>\n<ul class=\"contains-task-list\">\n<li class=\"task-list-item\"><input type=\"checkbox\" id=\"\" disabled=\"\" class=\"task-list-item-checkbox\" aria-label=\"Completed task\" checked=\"\"> I have read <a href=\"https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md\">CONTRIBUTING.md</a></li>\n<li class=\"task-list-item\"><input type=\"checkbox\" id=\"\" disabled=\"\" class=\"task-list-item-checkbox\" aria-label=\"Completed task\" checked=\"\"> I have read <a href=\"https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md\">CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h3 dir=\"auto\">Module(s)</h3>\n<p dir=\"auto\">mini.ai</p>\n<h3 dir=\"auto\">Description</h3>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Hi, following on from <a class=\"issue-link js-issue-link\" data-error-text=\"Failed to load title\" data-id=\"1617896584\" data-permission-text=\"Title is private\" data-url=\"https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/issues/261\" data-hovercard-type=\"issue\" data-hovercard-url=\"/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/issues/261/hovercard\" href=\"https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/issues/261\">#261</a> I have been attempting to create text objects for <code class=\"notranslate\">w</code>, <code class=\"notranslate\">W</code> and <code class=\"notranslate\">p</code>. I have made some headway with <code class=\"notranslate\">w</code>, it currently looks like this (updated: 20230316):</p>\n<div class=\"highlight highlight-source-lua notranslate position-relative overflow-auto\" dir=\"auto\" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content=\"          w = { '[^%w@_]*%f[%w@_]()()[%w@_]+()%s*()[^%w@_]*' },\"><pre class=\"notranslate\">          <span class=\"pl-smi\">w</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> { <span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>[^%w@_]*%f[%w@_]()()[%w@_]+()%s*()[^%w@_]*<span class=\"pl-pds\">' </span></span>},</pre></div>\n<p dir=\"auto\">The problem is when I input <code class=\"notranslate\">2yiw</code> for example, it seems to want to yank only the second \"word\". Default vim behaviour would be to select both the current \"word\" and the next one. I'm guessing this is to do with the way <code class=\"notranslate\">v:count</code> is implemented for text objects?</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">I don't know whether this is a bug or a feature, for example I know with bracket-type text objects, the behaviour of v:count with a count of 2 would be to select the brackets surrounding the ones you're in. (i.e. to expand outwards). But I think for things like <code class=\"notranslate\">word</code>s and <code class=\"notranslate\">WORD</code>s it makes sense to expand forwards.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">I'm wondering if it makes sense for text objects to be able to specify how they interact with <code class=\"notranslate\">v:count</code>?</p>",
    "bodyText": "Contributing guidelines\n\n I have read CONTRIBUTING.md\n I have read CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md\n\nModule(s)\nmini.ai\nDescription\nHi, following on from #261 I have been attempting to create text objects for w, W and p. I have made some headway with w, it currently looks like this (updated: 20230316):\n          w = { '[^%w@_]*%f[%w@_]()()[%w@_]+()%s*()[^%w@_]*' },\nThe problem is when I input 2yiw for example, it seems to want to yank only the second \"word\". Default vim behaviour would be to select both the current \"word\" and the next one. I'm guessing this is to do with the way v:count is implemented for text objects?\nI don't know whether this is a bug or a feature, for example I know with bracket-type text objects, the behaviour of v:count with a count of 2 would be to select the brackets surrounding the ones you're in. (i.e. to expand outwards). But I think for things like words and WORDs it makes sense to expand forwards.\nI'm wondering if it makes sense for text objects to be able to specify how they interact with v:count?",
