            "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> I'm wondering if it makes sense for text objects to be able to specify how they interact with `v:count`?\r\n\r\nTextobject interacts with `v:count` as if user consecutively selected it `v:count1` times. So `v3a)` is identical to `va)a)a)`.\r\n\r\nHow textobject behaves in consecutive selection depends on `search_method` and textobject specification itself. If `search_method` contains \"cover\" (like `cover`, `cover_or_next`, etc.), it will attempt to find smallest region which covers current selection (start is before, end is after) and *matches specification*.\r\n\r\nIn your case this pattern doesn't seem to match two words separated by whitespace (like `abc def`), so it can not find covering match. If you use default search method (`cover_or_next`), it will attempt to find *next* match (start is after, end is after) which is what happens from your description.\r\n\r\nI am honestly not sure how to make it better using Lua patterns. One other approach might be to use function which returns array of regions, but it might be computationally expensive and not trivial. ",
            "bodyHTML": "<blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">I'm wondering if it makes sense for text objects to be able to specify how they interact with <code class=\"notranslate\">v:count</code>?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Textobject interacts with <code class=\"notranslate\">v:count</code> as if user consecutively selected it <code class=\"notranslate\">v:count1</code> times. So <code class=\"notranslate\">v3a)</code> is identical to <code class=\"notranslate\">va)a)a)</code>.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">How textobject behaves in consecutive selection depends on <code class=\"notranslate\">search_method</code> and textobject specification itself. If <code class=\"notranslate\">search_method</code> contains \"cover\" (like <code class=\"notranslate\">cover</code>, <code class=\"notranslate\">cover_or_next</code>, etc.), it will attempt to find smallest region which covers current selection (start is before, end is after) and <em>matches specification</em>.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">In your case this pattern doesn't seem to match two words separated by whitespace (like <code class=\"notranslate\">abc def</code>), so it can not find covering match. If you use default search method (<code class=\"notranslate\">cover_or_next</code>), it will attempt to find <em>next</em> match (start is after, end is after) which is what happens from your description.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">I am honestly not sure how to make it better using Lua patterns. One other approach might be to use function which returns array of regions, but it might be computationally expensive and not trivial.</p>",
            "bodyText": "I'm wondering if it makes sense for text objects to be able to specify how they interact with v:count?\n\nTextobject interacts with v:count as if user consecutively selected it v:count1 times. So v3a) is identical to va)a)a).\nHow textobject behaves in consecutive selection depends on search_method and textobject specification itself. If search_method contains \"cover\" (like cover, cover_or_next, etc.), it will attempt to find smallest region which covers current selection (start is before, end is after) and matches specification.\nIn your case this pattern doesn't seem to match two words separated by whitespace (like abc def), so it can not find covering match. If you use default search method (cover_or_next), it will attempt to find next match (start is after, end is after) which is what happens from your description.\nI am honestly not sure how to make it better using Lua patterns. One other approach might be to use function which returns array of regions, but it might be computationally expensive and not trivial.",
