                    "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
                    "body": "> No worries. So do you think there's no scope for mini.ai to handle this kind of interaction? Or it just depends on implementation?\r\n\r\nFor more complex definitions of text object, there is a [function form](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/doc/mini-ai.txt#L240) instead of Lua patterns. It's API is a bit overcomplicated, but that is the price to allow flexibility without much extra source code. For more hands-on example see [argument textobject](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/lua/mini/ai.lua#L678).",
                    "bodyHTML": "<blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">No worries. So do you think there's no scope for mini.ai to handle this kind of interaction? Or it just depends on implementation?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">For more complex definitions of text object, there is a <a href=\"https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/doc/mini-ai.txt#L240\">function form</a> instead of Lua patterns. It's API is a bit overcomplicated, but that is the price to allow flexibility without much extra source code. For more hands-on example see <a href=\"https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/lua/mini/ai.lua#L678\">argument textobject</a>.</p>",
                    "bodyText": "No worries. So do you think there's no scope for mini.ai to handle this kind of interaction? Or it just depends on implementation?\n\nFor more complex definitions of text object, there is a function form instead of Lua patterns. It's API is a bit overcomplicated, but that is the price to allow flexibility without much extra source code. For more hands-on example see argument textobject.",
